fact WorkItem(id: "WI-869", description: some(value: "PER-PROVISION CONDITIONAL PROVISIONS — the per-clause form 058 §3.8 records and §4 folds into the provides-consolidation: `provides X[…] :- goals`, conditions scoped to ONE provision instead of the whole sort. DRIVEN BY A SHIPPED SHAPE (WI-858): `anthill.prelude.Pair` provides PartialEq AND Eq componentwise, and a sort has ONE `requires` chain shared by every provision it makes, so the two cannot be conditioned separately. MEASURED both ways at WI-858's HEAD: with the chain spelled `requires Eq[A], requires Eq[B]` (the shape WI-858 originally prescribed) `Pair[A = Float, B = Int64]` became a LOAD ERROR — Float provides NonEq and WI-835's check_use_site_requires_eq refuses a NonEq carrier at a parameter whose sort requires Eq — i.e. Pair stopped being a general PRODUCT, which is wrong: a pair of anything is a pair (unlike Set/Map, which genuinely need Eq on keys). Shipped instead with `requires PartialEq[A], requires PartialEq[B]`: Pair[Float, …] loads again, the componentwise eq body has exactly the evidence it needs, and PartialEq[Pair] is conditioned EXACTLY right. The residue this ticket owns: `provides Eq[Pair]` rides that same chain, so it over-claims — lawful equality is asserted where only the components' PARTIAL equality holds. The wanted spelling is `provides PartialEq[Pair] :- PartialEq[A], PartialEq[B]` beside `provides Eq[Pair] :- Eq[A], Eq[B]`. NOT A NEW MECHANISM per 058 §3.8: a per-provision chain is a second contributor to the dictionary's PROVIDER HALF, not a new half, and WI-857 settled the layout that makes it land somewhere. WORK: grammar (a per-clause `:- goals` tail on a provision), per-provision chains carried through SortProvidesInfo, check_provider_requires reading the owning clause's chain rather than the sort's, and dict_layout's provider half built from it. ACCEPTANCE: pair.anthill spells the two provisions separately; Pair[Float, Int64] still loads; a use demanding Eq[Pair[Float, Int64]] is REFUSED naming the unmet component condition; wi858_pair_orderings_test::a_pair_of_floats_still_loads keeps its meaning with its trade-note updated; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: 058 §3.8, §4; design/058-implementation.md §7 (the conditional-provisions paragraph); stdlib/anthill/prelude/pair.anthill's header."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-06T19:14:19Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-869", name: "modinst")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-869", author: "user", content: "A SECOND DRIVER, AND IT IS NOW A HARD BLOCKER: WI-877 depends on this ticket (edge added). WI-877 gives Pair its canonical lexicographic ordering, and MEASURED, it cannot be expressed at all without per-provision conditions — the ordering half works perfectly, but the requirement it needs poisons equality.\n\nWHAT WI-877 MEASURED, which extends this ticket's own measurement from Eq to Ordered. Adding 'requires Ordered[A], requires Ordered[B]' to Pair's chain (the only spelling the implementation accepts — see below) turns PartialEq.eq(pair(fst: 1.5, snd: 1), pair(fst: 1.5, snd: 1)) into the LOAD ERROR 'type mismatch in anthill.prelude.PartialEq.eq.dispatch: expected matching impl for per-call bindings, got no impl matches'. CONTROL: the same program on today's pair.anthill loads clean and evals Int(1). So the shared chain does not merely OVER-CLAIM here as it does for Eq[Pair] — it actively BREAKS a working operation, because Float provides PartialOrd and not Ordered.\n\nTHE OTHER TWO SPELLINGS ARE REFUSED, which is what makes this a technical prerequisite rather than a preference. Op-scoped 'requires Ordered[A], Ordered[B]' on Pair.compare: refused, \"overrides 'anthill.prelude.Ordered.compare' but does not refine it: it strengthens the precondition\" — an OVERRIDE cannot add a requirement its spec op lacks (which is why ordered.anthill's op-scoped requires on PartialOrd.gt is fine: that is a DECLARATION). No requirement at all: refused, 'expected requires Ordered[...] covering abstract type parameter, got missing requires Ordered[T = ...] on enclosing sort'. Naming the slots changes nothing.\n\nTWO THINGS THIS ADDS TO THE WORK/ACCEPTANCE HERE. (1) The failing path is the DISPATCH one — 'per-call bindings' in PartialEq.eq.dispatch — not only check_provider_requires. Whatever reads the sort's chain when threading a dispatch through the carrier has to read the OWNING PROVISION's chain too, or Pair will still demand Ordered at every eq. (2) The wanted end state for pair.anthill is FOUR conditioned provisions, not two: 'provides PartialEq[Pair] :- PartialEq[A], PartialEq[B]', 'provides Eq[Pair] :- Eq[A], Eq[B]', 'provides PartialOrd[Pair] :- PartialOrd[A], PartialOrd[B]', 'provides Ordered[Pair] :- Ordered[A], Ordered[B]' — and the acceptance arm that discriminates is that a Pair[Float, Int64] still COMPARES FOR EQUALITY (evaluates, not merely loads) while a total comparison of it is refused naming the unmet Ordered[Float]. REFERENCE: WI-877's feedback carries the full measurement including what the ordering half already does correctly.", at: "2026-08-05T02:31:29Z")
